---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Command from '../components/Command.astro';
import { url, repository } from '../lib/site';
---
<Layout title="Install Pinak — Windows, macOS & Linux" description="Install pinak-cli with pipx or a virtual environment, check your setup, and download your first media file.">
  <div class="wrap"><section class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">GET PINAK / START LOCAL</span><h1>Your next<br/><span style="color:var(--accent)">favorite command.</span></h1><p>Install Pinak in its own environment, check your tools, and make your first download. No account required.</p></section><div class="content-narrow" style="margin-left:0"><div class="callout"><strong>Before you begin:</strong> Pinak supports Python 3.10 or newer. The current pipx release needs Python 3.11 or newer; with Python 3.10, use the virtual-environment option below or a compatible distribution-packaged pipx. Downloading needs network access to the source. FFmpeg is used for merging, conversion, audio extraction, and media playback; some operations can provision it automatically.</div>
  <p>These guides follow the repository source. The <a href="https://pypi.org/project/pinak-cli/">published PyPI release</a> can be older, so use <code>pinak --version</code> and <code>pinak --help</code> to confirm which commands your installation provides.</p>
  <h2>01 / Choose your system</h2><p>Use pipx for an isolated installation. After setting up pipx, close and reopen your terminal so its commands are available.</p><div class="os-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Operating system"><button id="tab-windows" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-windows" type="button">Windows</button><button id="tab-macos" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-macos" tabindex="-1" type="button">macOS</button><button id="tab-linux" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-linux" tabindex="-1" type="button">Linux</button></div>
  <section class="os-panel" id="panel-windows" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-windows" tabindex="0"><h3>Windows / PowerShell</h3><p>Install Python from <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/windows/">python.org</a>, including its launcher. Then set up pipx:</p><Command command="py -m pip install --user pipx"/><Command command="py -m pipx ensurepath"/><p>Reopen PowerShell, then install:</p><Command command="pipx install pinak-cli"/></section>
  <section class="os-panel" id="panel-macos" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-macos" tabindex="0"><h3>macOS / Terminal</h3><p>With <a href="https://brew.sh/">Homebrew</a> installed, set up Python and pipx:</p><Command command="brew install python pipx"/><Command command="pipx ensurepath"/><p>Reopen Terminal, then install:</p><Command command="pipx install pinak-cli"/></section>
  <section class="os-panel" id="panel-linux" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-linux" tabindex="0"><h3>Linux / Debian & Ubuntu</h3><p>These commands target Debian/Ubuntu. For another distribution, use its package manager to install Python 3.10+ and pipx.</p><Command command="sudo apt update"/><Command command="sudo apt install python3 python3-venv pipx"/><Command command="pipx ensurepath"/><p>Reopen your terminal, then install:</p><Command command="pipx install pinak-cli"/></section>
  <h2>02 / Make sure you’re ready</h2><Command command="pinak --version"/><Command command="pinak --help"/><Command command="pinak --doctor"/><p>Doctor checks tools, storage, and connectivity. A connectivity warning does not necessarily mean every source is unreachable.</p>
  <h2>03 / Make your first download</h2><p>Replace the placeholder with a full supported URL for media you own or have permission to save:</p><Command command={'pinak "<your-media-url>"'}/><p>Choose a quality and folder:</p><Command command={'pinak "<your-media-url>" --quality 720p -o downloads'}/><p>Or extract audio:</p><Command command={'pinak "<your-media-url>" --audio'}/><p>Run <code>pinak</code> without a URL to open the interactive shell.</p>
  <h2>Optional / Local playback</h2><p>Desktop playback needs a working audio device. Native windows also need a graphical desktop and Tkinter or optional OpenCV. These are separate from downloading.</p><p>On Debian/Ubuntu, using the distribution’s Python:</p><Command command="sudo apt install ffmpeg libportaudio2 python3-tk"/><p>On Windows/macOS, use a Python installation with Tkinter. SoundDevice wheels include PortAudio on those platforms. If choosing OpenCV as an alternative native-window backend:</p><Command command="pipx inject pinak-cli opencv-python"/><p><a href={url('/docs/playback/')}>Read playback setup and controls →</a></p>
  <h2>Alternative / Virtual environment</h2><p>Use an isolated environment if you prefer pip. On Windows:</p><Command command="py -m venv .venv"/><Command command=".\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1"/><p>On macOS/Linux:</p><Command command="python3 -m venv .venv"/><Command command="source .venv/bin/activate"/><p>Then, in your activated environment:</p><Command command="python -m pip install pinak-cli"/>
  <h2>Alternative / From source</h2><Command command={`git clone ${repository}.git`}/><Command command="cd Pinak-CLI"/><p>Create and activate a virtual environment as above, then:</p><Command command="python -m pip install ./cli"/><div class="callout">Repository source and the latest published PyPI package can differ. Check <code>pinak --version</code> after installation. <a href="https://pypi.org/project/pinak-cli/">View published releases ↗</a></div><div class="guide-links"><a class="button" href={url('/docs/')}>Read the documentation →</a><a class="button button-ghost" href={url('/docs/troubleshooting/')}>Need a hand?</a></div></div></div>
</Layout>
